    "slug": "offshoreoutsourcingcompany-how-much-of-the-executive-meeting-packet-is-traceable-to-a-source-2026-11-02",
    "title": "How much of the executive meeting packet is traceable to a source?",
    "excerpt": "A documentary study of meeting-preparation packets, testing whether each material fact, open question, and recommendation can be traced to an approved source and a named owner.",
    "date": "2026-11-02",
    "modifiedDate": "2026-11-02",
    "heading": "Research question",
    "hero": "/thumbnails/november-02/offshoreoutsourcingcompany-how-much-of-the-executive-meeting-packet-is-traceable-to-a-source-2026-11-02.webp",
    "body": [
      "Question and scope",
      "This study asks what share of the material statements in an executive meeting packet can be traced to a source that a reviewer can open. It also asks whether open questions and recommendations carry a named owner and a date. The unit is one packet version prepared for one meeting. The purpose is to test preparation quality, not to judge the executive's decision or the assistant's diligence. The scope includes the packet cover, the decision summary, the fact list, the open-question list, and the recommendation section. It excludes the meeting itself and any verbal briefing that was not captured in the record. A packet that relies on a verbal briefing may still be useful, but its traceability cannot be tested from the document alone.",
      "Method and evidence frame",
      "Use a document-level coding method. For each material statement, record whether it is a fact, an inference, a recommendation, or an open question. For a fact, record the source name, the date observed, and whether the reviewer could open the source. For an inference, record the facts it depends on. For a recommendation, record the owner and the decision it supports. For an open question, record the owner and the date an answer is needed. The method separates the packet from the underlying record. A statement is not traceable merely because it sounds specific. The reviewer must be able to reach the source without asking the author to explain it. If the source is a person, record the person's role and the date of the conversation, and note that a verbal source is weaker than a signed record for a material fact.",
      "Population and unit of analysis",
      "The population is all meeting packets prepared for a defined set of executives or decision forums during the observation window. The unit is one packet version. A revised packet is a separate version but is linked to the original so the reviewer can see what changed. A meeting with no packet is outside the unit definition but is recorded as a missing-preparation case if the approved process required one. Define the materiality rule before sampling. A material statement is one that could change a decision, a commitment, a cost, a legal position, or a people action. Routine scheduling details may be excluded. Record the rule and apply it consistently. If two reviewers disagree about materiality, preserve the disagreement and use it to refine the rule for the next sample.",
      "Definitions and measures",
      "The primary measure is the share of material facts with an openable source and a date. A secondary measure is the share of material recommendations with a named owner and a stated decision. A third measure is the share of open questions with an owner and a due date. Report counts, denominators, and the definition used. A single traceability score can hide the difference between a missing source and a source that exists but is not linked. Supporting measures include the number of conflicting sources, the number of statements that are actually inferences presented as facts, the age of the oldest source, and the share of packets that contain a confidentiality handling note. These measures describe the preparation process rather than the quality of the eventual decision.",
      "Sampling and collection",
      "Sample across meeting types, executives, and preparation lead times. Include at least one recurring meeting and one high-stakes ad hoc meeting. Include a revised packet if one exists. Record the sampling frame and every exclusion. A sample drawn only from well-prepared recurring meetings will not show how the process behaves under time pressure. Collect the packet version, the meeting invitation, the prior decision record, the source links, and the distribution list. Record the collection time and whether the packet was the version actually used in the meeting. If the packet was distributed by email, preserve the message headers or the export that shows the recipients and the time. Do not reconstruct a packet after the meeting from memory.",
      "Analysis and reporting",
      "Report traceability by section and by statement type. A packet may have excellent source coverage in the fact list but no owners in the recommendation section. Show the pattern rather than only the total. Group defects by missing source, broken link, undated source, inference presented as fact, missing owner, and missing due date. Note which defects are concentrated in particular meeting types. Separate observation from interpretation. A missing source does not prove the statement is false; it proves the reviewer cannot verify it from the packet. A high traceability rate on a small packet does not prove that the preparation was complete if the packet omitted a material issue entirely. The study can measure what is present, but it cannot measure what was never written down without a separate completeness check.",
      "Limits and sources of error",
      "Executive packets often contain sensitive material, so the reviewer may not have access to every source. Record the access limitation rather than scoring an inaccessible source as missing. The reviewer's own knowledge of the business can make a statement seem traceable when the link is actually ambiguous. Use a reviewer who is independent of the packet but authorized for the material. The study cannot establish that the meeting made a good decision, that the assistant exercised sound judgment, or that the executive was well prepared in a way that is not visible in the document. It tests the document's traceability. Public records-management and privacy guidance provides general context, not a standard for a specific company.",
      "Operational use",
      "Use the result to simplify the packet template, improve the source-link workflow, or add a required owner field. A recurring defect in one section is usually a template problem. A defect concentrated in one meeting type may require a different preparation lead time or a clearer intake from the meeting owner. Choose one change and re-test the same section after the change. Traceability has a practical cost: it takes time to link sources. The study should report that cost alongside the benefit so the owner can decide how much evidence is proportionate to the meeting's risk. A low-risk recurring meeting may need fewer links than a board decision. The point is to make the choice explicit rather than applying one standard to every packet.",
      "Review cadence",
      "Review the packet process quarterly and after a change to the template, the source systems, or the executive team. Keep the materiality rule stable across the comparison. If the rule changes, report the change and do not compare the new score directly with the old one. Close the review with one owner, one template or workflow change, and a re-test date. Preserve the sampled packets and the coding sheet so a later reviewer can repeat the method. A process that cannot be re-tested is itself a traceability problem.",
      "Sources: https://www.sba.gov/ https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework https://www.dol.gov/"
    ],
